1
00:00:01,820 --> 00:00:02,300
948.

2
00:00:02,530 --> 00:00:04,820
That's how many separate ways an AI found

3
00:00:05,050 --> 00:00:07,580
to take over a Linux
computer from the inside.

4
00:00:08,418 --> 00:00:09,938
No stolen password.

5
00:00:10,538 --> 00:00:12,078
No remote break-in.

6
00:00:12,278 --> 00:00:15,698
Each one starts with an ordinary user
who has no special rights,

7
00:00:15,928 --> 00:00:19,018
and ends with root,
the account that can do anything.

8
00:00:19,695 --> 00:00:23,635
OpenAI published the result
in November 2026.

9
00:00:24,335 --> 00:00:28,375
The whole run cost
$1.2 million in API credits.

10
00:00:29,137 --> 00:00:32,997
These are OpenAI's own numbers,
published the day before it launched

11
00:00:33,227 --> 00:00:37,917
the model that produced them, and nobody
outside has confirmed the full count.

12
00:00:38,637 --> 00:00:42,177
But hundreds of reports and patches
have already gone out.

13
00:00:42,761 --> 00:00:44,901
Here's why this matters beyond Linux.

14
00:00:45,781 --> 00:00:49,581
The Linux source code is public.
Anyone can read it.

15
00:00:50,101 --> 00:00:53,041
For decades, that was the argument
for why it's safe.

16
00:00:53,443 --> 00:00:55,163
So if a machine can read public code

17
00:00:55,393 --> 00:00:58,223
and turn it into working
exploits at this scale,

18
00:00:58,383 --> 00:01:01,243
should software keep
its code secret after all?

19
00:01:02,352 --> 00:01:04,652
Let's look at software that already does.

20
00:01:05,532 --> 00:01:12,292
In April 2026, Microsoft fixed 164
security flaws on its monthly patch day.

21
00:01:12,812 --> 00:01:14,812
In October, it fixed 1,413.

22
00:01:17,151 --> 00:01:21,191
In that single month, it fixed more
than in any whole year before it.

23
00:01:21,651 --> 00:01:25,811
Over 1,000 of those fixes were in Windows,
and Microsoft had warned

24
00:01:26,041 --> 00:01:28,771
that its own AI tools
would drive the surge.

25
00:01:29,473 --> 00:01:31,973
You can't compare
the two numbers directly.

26
00:01:32,573 --> 00:01:37,173
Companies count differently, and Microsoft
was searching its own private code.

27
00:01:37,527 --> 00:01:39,247
But it does show one thing.

28
00:01:39,647 --> 00:01:42,487
Keeping the code private
had not kept the bugs out.

29
00:01:44,767 --> 00:01:46,687
So what actually protects you?

30
00:01:47,367 --> 00:01:50,207
To answer that,
we need to see how this machine works,

31
00:01:50,287 --> 00:01:52,807
because every step
of it throws something away.

32
00:01:53,462 --> 00:01:56,822
The Linux kernel has
more than 40 million lines of code.

33
00:01:57,742 --> 00:02:01,122
First, scout agents went through it
and picked out the parts

34
00:02:01,352 --> 00:02:03,602
an ordinary user can actually reach.

35
00:02:04,012 --> 00:02:06,352
That left about 7%.

36
00:02:06,732 --> 00:02:09,132
It's the part they judged worth attacking,

37
00:02:09,212 --> 00:02:13,152
not a random sample,
so you can't multiply the result by 14.

38
00:02:13,859 --> 00:02:18,019
Next, analyst agents went through that
code and listed everything suspicious.

39
00:02:18,249 --> 00:02:22,839
2,308 candidates, in 12 hours.

40
00:02:23,172 --> 00:02:25,132
But a suspicion proves nothing.

41
00:02:25,832 --> 00:02:30,012
So other agents wrote test programs
and ran them on isolated virtual machines.

42
00:02:30,242 --> 00:02:35,312
1,724 bugs turned out to be real.

43
00:02:35,723 --> 00:02:39,423
Then came the expensive part:
building working exploits,

44
00:02:39,503 --> 00:02:42,763
with the standard protections
of a normal desktop system switched on.

45
00:02:42,993 --> 00:02:47,243
1,169 reached root.

46
00:02:47,874 --> 00:02:52,654
Group those by the underlying flaw,
and you get the 948.

47
00:02:53,703 --> 00:02:56,863
There's one detail in the report
that's hard to forget.

48
00:02:57,663 --> 00:03:00,983
In an early run,
the agents reported 200 successes.

49
00:03:01,883 --> 00:03:04,303
Then humans checked
how they had got there.

50
00:03:04,811 --> 00:03:08,171
Twenty of them had reached root
through a different unknown bug,

51
00:03:08,251 --> 00:03:10,691
not the one they had been told to use.

52
00:03:11,111 --> 00:03:15,171
Sent to open one door,
they found another that nobody knew about.

53
00:03:15,832 --> 00:03:20,972
In a sample of its exploits, OpenAI
identified ten exploitation techniques

54
00:03:21,202 --> 00:03:23,812
that outside experts hadn't seen before.

55
00:03:24,440 --> 00:03:28,950
In July, an earlier model
found 166 of these flaws.

56
00:03:29,260 --> 00:03:33,460
Three months of fixes later,
the newer model found 948.

57
00:03:33,540 --> 00:03:35,540
The bugs hadn't run out.

58
00:03:36,060 --> 00:03:38,040
The later run found far more.

59
00:03:40,330 --> 00:03:42,430
Now, there's an important limit.

60
00:03:43,090 --> 00:03:46,050
None of this breaks into your computer
over the internet.

61
00:03:46,710 --> 00:03:50,810
The attacker already has
to be on the machine, as an ordinary user.

62
00:03:51,417 --> 00:03:52,777
That's less comforting than it sounds,

63
00:03:53,007 --> 00:03:55,957
because real attacks
often come in two steps.

64
00:03:56,417 --> 00:04:00,237
First you get in, through a browser
flaw or a malicious download.

65
00:04:00,897 --> 00:04:04,597
Then you need root.
This is the second step.

66
00:04:05,172 --> 00:04:07,512
OpenAI's advice is blunt.

67
00:04:08,152 --> 00:04:12,692
On a system without the latest patches,
treat any ordinary account as root,

68
00:04:12,772 --> 00:04:14,952
at least against a well-funded attacker.

69
00:04:17,522 --> 00:04:20,642
So where does that leave
the old argument for open code?

70
00:04:21,342 --> 00:04:24,582
In the late nineties,
Eric Raymond gave it a slogan:

71
00:04:24,922 --> 00:04:27,362
"Given enough eyeballs,
all bugs are shallow."

72
00:04:28,031 --> 00:04:29,711
He called it Linus's Law.

73
00:04:30,051 --> 00:04:33,411
It's a lovely idea,
with one assumption inside it:

74
00:04:33,491 --> 00:04:35,531
that somebody is actually looking.

75
00:04:36,150 --> 00:04:40,370
In 2014, a bug called Heartbleed
turned up in OpenSSL,

76
00:04:40,850 --> 00:04:43,590
the code that protected
a huge share of the web.

77
00:04:44,610 --> 00:04:47,970
About half a million secure servers
were thought to be exposed.

78
00:04:48,813 --> 00:04:51,833
The bug had sat in public
code for two years.

79
00:04:52,433 --> 00:04:55,513
The project was getting
about $2,000 a year in donations,

80
00:04:55,743 --> 00:04:58,533
and had one full-time developer.

81
00:04:59,279 --> 00:05:00,939
Anyone could read it.

82
00:05:01,199 --> 00:05:02,759
Hardly anyone did.

83
00:05:03,652 --> 00:05:06,452
That was always the real
weakness of open source.

84
00:05:07,192 --> 00:05:10,232
Not too many eyes on the code. Too few.

85
00:05:10,556 --> 00:05:12,616
And that's the part AI changes.

86
00:05:13,456 --> 00:05:16,636
A machine can keep scanning,
and investigate far more candidates

87
00:05:16,866 --> 00:05:19,436
than a human researcher
could handle alone.

88
00:05:20,296 --> 00:05:24,616
The old promise is finally being kept,
for everybody, attackers included.

89
00:05:27,156 --> 00:05:30,196
So public code really
does get examined now.

90
00:05:30,876 --> 00:05:32,156
Does hiding it help?

91
00:05:32,801 --> 00:05:35,081
A closed program still has to run,

92
00:05:35,161 --> 00:05:38,761
so its machine code sits
on every computer that uses it.

93
00:05:39,181 --> 00:05:43,041
Tools like Ghidra turn that back
into something like source code.

94
00:05:43,372 --> 00:05:44,932
And there's a classic trick.

95
00:05:45,732 --> 00:05:48,712
Compare a program
before and after a security update.

96
00:05:49,392 --> 00:05:52,032
Whatever changed shows
you where the hole was.

97
00:05:52,409 --> 00:05:54,669
That tells attackers where to aim,

98
00:05:54,749 --> 00:05:57,749
well before every system
has installed the update.

99
00:05:58,479 --> 00:06:01,719
Anthropic tried this on the Windows
kernel, with 21 flaws

100
00:06:01,799 --> 00:06:05,199
Microsoft had recently patched,
and no source code.

101
00:06:06,039 --> 00:06:08,919
The AI's first crash came in 31 minutes.

102
00:06:09,599 --> 00:06:11,799
It had 18 within six hours.

103
00:06:12,445 --> 00:06:15,185
Eight became working exploits
capable of taking full

104
00:06:15,415 --> 00:06:19,285
control of the machine,
at roughly $2,000 each.

105
00:06:19,729 --> 00:06:21,849
But these weren't unknown bugs.

106
00:06:22,429 --> 00:06:25,209
The patches already pointed
to where the problems were.

107
00:06:25,789 --> 00:06:28,309
Finding a new flaw from scratch is harder.

108
00:06:28,639 --> 00:06:31,219
So secrecy still costs
an attacker something.

109
00:06:32,019 --> 00:06:35,719
It's a speed bump, not a wall.
How high is the bump?

110
00:06:36,039 --> 00:06:37,639
There's no public number.

111
00:06:39,949 --> 00:06:41,529
What about the other side?

112
00:06:42,109 --> 00:06:44,329
What does being open actually buy you?

113
00:06:44,949 --> 00:06:48,529
The answer is in a small detail
of OpenAI's report.

114
00:06:49,329 --> 00:06:51,829
During the ten days
of that scan and analysis,

115
00:06:52,109 --> 00:06:56,989
over 100 of these Linux flaws were fixed,
or about to be, by other people.

116
00:06:57,529 --> 00:07:01,409
About 300 more
were already being discussed in public.

117
00:07:02,120 --> 00:07:05,400
Other people were finding the same bugs
at the same time.

118
00:07:06,349 --> 00:07:09,129
Linus Torvalds had
described this back in May.

119
00:07:10,089 --> 00:07:13,289
He said AI reports had made
the kernel's security mailing list

120
00:07:13,519 --> 00:07:17,709
"almost entirely unmanageable",
because different people kept finding

121
00:07:17,939 --> 00:07:20,029
the same things with the same tools.

122
00:07:20,462 --> 00:07:24,462
And his advice was this:
"If you found a bug using AI tools,

123
00:07:24,542 --> 00:07:26,982
the chances are somebody
else found it too."

124
00:07:27,555 --> 00:07:29,995
The kernel's rules now say it outright.

125
00:07:30,935 --> 00:07:35,115
A bug found with AI must be treated
as public, because these bugs surface

126
00:07:35,345 --> 00:07:38,755
across several researchers,
often on the same day.

127
00:07:39,344 --> 00:07:42,604
Think about what that does
to the idea of a secret bug.

128
00:07:43,344 --> 00:07:46,484
If it was cheap for you to find,
it was cheap for everyone.

129
00:07:47,143 --> 00:07:50,063
With open source,
anyone can inspect the report,

130
00:07:50,143 --> 00:07:52,843
verify the fix, and challenge the result.

131
00:07:53,643 --> 00:07:56,643
With closed software,
you mostly have to trust the vendor.

132
00:07:59,363 --> 00:08:02,703
But openness has a bill,
and it isn't the one you'd expect.

133
00:08:03,343 --> 00:08:06,983
It's tempting to say
a small project can't afford the scanning.

134
00:08:07,723 --> 00:08:11,363
AI companies are already
offering maintainers free access.

135
00:08:12,114 --> 00:08:13,554
What's short is people.

136
00:08:14,254 --> 00:08:18,154
The kernel used to log
about 500 security issues per release.

137
00:08:18,974 --> 00:08:20,714
This year it passed 1,500.

138
00:08:21,654 --> 00:08:24,794
Many are minor,
but somebody still has to go through them.

139
00:08:25,293 --> 00:08:28,393
In January, the curl project
ended its paid bug bounty,

140
00:08:28,623 --> 00:08:31,553
to stop rewarding a flood of junk reports.

141
00:08:32,408 --> 00:08:34,588
Even good reports only
move the work along.

142
00:08:34,818 --> 00:08:39,248
AI-generated patches
still need human review.

143
00:08:39,888 --> 00:08:45,028
Some early fixes added unnecessary code
or missed other versions of the same bug.

144
00:08:45,628 --> 00:08:47,188
You can buy more scanning.

145
00:08:47,848 --> 00:08:51,408
You can't buy more people
who understand a piece of code that well.

146
00:08:51,928 --> 00:08:56,068
Here, a company with a paid security team
really does have an edge.

147
00:08:58,628 --> 00:09:00,568
And then comes the last problem.

148
00:09:01,188 --> 00:09:03,288
Fixed is not the same as installed.

149
00:09:04,059 --> 00:09:07,759
In March 2017,
Microsoft patched a flaw in Windows.

150
00:09:08,579 --> 00:09:11,779
Closed source, one vendor,
one update system.

151
00:09:12,479 --> 00:09:15,599
Two months later,
WannaCry went through that same hole,

152
00:09:15,829 --> 00:09:20,319
into some 200,000 computers
in about 150 countries.

153
00:09:20,755 --> 00:09:24,195
That patch had two months to spread,
and it wasn't enough.

154
00:09:24,755 --> 00:09:27,395
An attacker may no longer
leave you two months.

155
00:09:28,075 --> 00:09:31,415
In the same Anthropic study,
the AI took a public fix

156
00:09:31,645 --> 00:09:36,755
for Firefox's JavaScript engine, and had
a working exploit in under an hour.

157
00:09:37,303 --> 00:09:40,683
The fixed version of Firefox
was still 18 days away.

158
00:09:41,525 --> 00:09:44,005
This is the danger of publishing a fix.

159
00:09:44,765 --> 00:09:48,565
The information can reach attackers
before the update reaches you.

160
00:09:49,089 --> 00:09:51,049
A Linux fix has a long way to travel,

161
00:09:51,279 --> 00:09:55,649
from the kernel to a stable release,
to your distribution, to your device.

162
00:09:56,609 --> 00:09:58,929
A phone may wait months, or forever.

163
00:09:59,234 --> 00:10:01,734
And closed software doesn't escape this.

164
00:10:02,434 --> 00:10:05,054
We just saw it:
the Windows patch was enough.

165
00:10:05,914 --> 00:10:07,894
Every update is now also a map.

166
00:10:08,793 --> 00:10:11,973
So keep everything up to date,
your computer, your phone,

167
00:10:12,053 --> 00:10:15,533
even your router, and install
updates as soon as they arrive.

168
00:10:16,573 --> 00:10:19,053
As AI gets better at turning
fixes into attacks,

169
00:10:19,283 --> 00:10:21,693
every day you wait will matter more.

170
00:10:24,593 --> 00:10:29,193
So, is open source still safer?
Honestly, nobody knows.

171
00:10:29,993 --> 00:10:32,373
As far as is publicly known,
no one has run

172
00:10:32,603 --> 00:10:35,333
the same test on both sides,
under the same rules.

173
00:10:36,042 --> 00:10:37,402
What we can say is more modest.

174
00:10:37,612 --> 00:10:41,142
Hiding code buys less than it used to.

175
00:10:41,222 --> 00:10:43,622
Showing it lets anyone check the work,

176
00:10:43,852 --> 00:10:47,562
and hands a bigger pile of that work
to the people who maintain it.

177
00:10:48,689 --> 00:10:51,329
Even if AI never finds every bug,

178
00:10:51,409 --> 00:10:54,429
it has already changed
the economics of finding them.

179
00:10:55,150 --> 00:10:57,550
What matters now is how
long a hole stays open

180
00:10:57,780 --> 00:10:59,350
between the moment somebody finds it

181
00:10:59,580 --> 00:11:02,170
and the moment it's closed
on your machine.

182
00:11:05,480 --> 00:11:07,320
So here's a question for you.

183
00:11:07,580 --> 00:11:10,620
If any bug can be found by anyone,
would you rather trust

184
00:11:10,850 --> 00:11:14,240
code you can read,
or a company that's paid to fix it?

185
00:11:14,980 --> 00:11:16,270
Let me know in the comments.
